  "origin_note": "Candidate historical family centered on Ancient Greek Τῑμόθεος (Timótheos) and its Latin, Romance, Germanic, Slavic and other descendants/adaptations listed by the pinned Wiktionary entries.",
  "etymology_note": "Pinned Wiktionary revisions trace English Timothy through Middle French Timothée and Latin Tīmotheus to Ancient Greek Τῑμόθεος, which the entries analyze as τῑμάω ('to honour') + θεός ('god'). The Spanish, Catalan, Portuguese, Polish and Finnish entries derive their forms from Latin Tīmotheus (itself from the Greek name); the Ukrainian entry derives its form directly from Ancient Greek, the Armenian entry through Old Armenian and the Romanian entry through Old Church Slavonic. The German Tim/Timo entries also describe those forms as diminutives of Dietmar, a different name that is not part of this family. This is etymological history, not a promise that every modern bearer shares one literal 'name meaning'.",
  "meaning_note": "Do not collapse local full-name equivalents, borrowed spellings, transliterations, or short/pet forms. Tim, Timo and similar forms are separate-set candidates, not substitutes for full-name forms. Character localization and user-name localization require their own policy/consent layer.",
